   "level": 3,
   "statement": "<p>Children stand in a line, and you know their heights. For each child, find the first child standing <em>after</em> them (to their right) who is <strong>strictly taller</strong>, and print that child's height. If nobody to the right is taller, print <code>-1</code> for that child.</p>",
   "input": "<p>The first line holds <code>n</code>. The second line holds the <code>n</code> heights, from the front of the line to the back.</p>",
   "output": "<p>One line with <code>n</code> integers separated by single spaces.</p>",
   "constraints": "<p>1 ≤ n ≤ 1000; 1 ≤ height ≤ 300.</p>",
   "examples": [
    {
     "in": "6\n2 7 3 5 4 6\n",
     "out": "7 -1 5 6 6 -1\n"
    }
   ],
   "hint": "<p>For each child, scan to the right and stop at the first taller one. (There is also a faster way: walk from the <em>back</em> of the line and keep a list of children who could still be the answer for someone.)</p>",
   "solution": "n = int(input())\nh = list(map(int, input().split()))\nans = [-1] * n\nstack = []                 # heights to the right that could still be an answer, tallest at the bottom\nfor i in range(n - 1, -1, -1):\n    while stack and stack[-1] <= h[i]:\n        stack.pop()\n    if stack:\n        ans[i] = stack[-1]\n    stack.append(h[i])\nprint(*ans)",
